+## Quick comparison
+
+| Attribute | Alder AirLite | Bracken PowerMax |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Price | $219 | $249 |
+| Weight | 5.7 lb | 7.2 lb |
+| Maximum runtime | 42 minutes | 58 minutes |
+| Dust-bin capacity | 0.45 L | 0.70 L |
+| Filtration | Sealed HEPA; 99.97% capture to 0.3 microns | Washable five-stage filter; not sealed |
+| Noise | 74 dB | 78 dB |
+
+## Trade-offs
+
+- **Alder AirLite:** Its sealed HEPA filtration directly suits a home with an indoor cat, while the soft-roller design is appropriate for mostly hard floors and its 5.7-pound body is easier to handle. Its 42-minute maximum runtime and 0.45-liter bin trail Bracken, but both should remain workable for a 950-square-foot apartment with two area rugs; it is also $30 cheaper and 4 dB quieter.
+- **Bracken PowerMax:** The 58-minute runtime offers 16 extra minutes and the 0.70-liter bin reduces emptying during a full-home clean. Those endurance gains serve the second-ranked concern, yet its washable five-stage filter is not sealed, a meaningful drawback for the first-ranked filtration need; it is also 1.5 pounds heavier, 4 dB louder, and $30 more expensive.
+
+## Recommendation
+
+Choose the Alder AirLite because its sealed HEPA system best serves the first-ranked filtration need around an indoor cat, while its 42-minute runtime should be practical for a 950-square-foot, mostly hard-floor apartment with two rugs. Bracken runs longer, but that second-ranked runtime advantage does not outweigh Alder's documented particle-capture performance, easier handling, lower noise, and lower price.
